    "stimulusGroupId": "aplang-g-w-fridge",
    "stimulus": {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Draft proposal: A Community Refrigerator",
      "text": "(1) Our neighborhood has generous people and wasted food. (2) Restaurants sometimes discard safe surplus at closing, while residents a few blocks away struggle to assemble a meal. (3) A community refrigerator, placed outside the recreation center and open during center hours, could connect those two facts.\n\n(4) The city should provide the weatherproof shelter and electrical connection. (5) A volunteer team would post clear donation rules, check temperatures twice daily, and remove expired items. (6) Similar projects require dependable routines; goodwill alone does not keep food safe. (7) For that reason, the proposal includes a rotating inspection schedule and names the center manager as the final point of accountability.\n\n(8) Some neighbors may worry that the refrigerator will attract litter or place too much work on volunteers. (9) Those concerns deserve a three-month pilot rather than a permanent commitment. (10) During the pilot, volunteers would record donations, removals, temperature checks, cleanup time, and complaints. (11) The council could then decide from local evidence whether the project reduces waste without burdening the center.\n\n(12) A refrigerator cannot solve food insecurity. (13) It can, however, keep usable food out of the trash and make one small form of mutual aid reliable. (14) Approving a measured pilot would let the neighborhood test that promise responsibly.",
      "source": "Original passage created for AP Exam Practice."
    },
