    "stimulusGroupId": "aplang-g-w-start",
    "stimulus": {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Draft argument: Testing a Later School Start",
      "text": "(1) The first bell at North Valley High rings at 7:20 a.m. (2) Some students arrive alert, while others begin class after long bus rides and too little sleep. (3) Next year, the district should test an 8:10 start at North Valley for one semester before deciding whether to change schedules districtwide.\n\n(4) A later start will not automatically improve every grade. (5) It does create a condition researchers associate with adolescent sleep needs: more opportunity to sleep at a biologically appropriate time. (6) During the pilot, the district should compare attendance, first-period tardiness, nurse visits, course performance, and student sleep surveys with the same semester from prior years.\n\n(7) Transportation and athletics make the change difficult. (8) The district can reduce disruption by keeping elementary routes unchanged, shifting one shared bus tier, and scheduling practices forty minutes later where facilities allow. (9) The pilot should also track late-bus costs and missed practice time so benefits are not considered in isolation.\n\n(10) One semester cannot settle every question, especially because seasonal activities vary. (11) Yet continuing the current schedule provides no neutral control; it simply preserves a choice made under different assumptions. (12) A defined pilot, published measures, and a public review would replace speculation with evidence the district can use.",
      "source": "Original passage created for AP Exam Practice."
    },
    "sequence": 0,
    "q": "Which sentence would best precede sentence 1 to establish the broader rhetorical situation?",
