    "stimulusGroupId": "aplang-g-w-history",
    "stimulus": {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Draft recommendation: An Oral-History Archive",
      "text": "(1) The library owns boxes of photographs from the mills that closed in the 1980s. (2) Names appear on only a few envelopes, and each year fewer residents can identify the workers, streets, and machines. (3) The library should begin a two-year oral-history project while that knowledge can still be connected to the images.\n\n(4) Staff members cannot conduct every interview. (5) They can train volunteers to use a common consent form, recording checklist, and question guide. (6) The first workshop should include practice interviews and instruction on the difference between a memory and a verified historical fact. (7) Catalog records can preserve both by labeling personal recollections clearly and linking them to corroborating documents when available.\n\n(8) Digitization is not the same as access. (9) Files need searchable names, dates, subjects, transcripts, and permissions; otherwise the archive becomes a new box that happens to glow. (10) The project budget should therefore prioritize description and transcription alongside recording equipment.\n\n(11) Interviewers will sometimes hear conflicting accounts. (12) The archive should retain those conflicts rather than silently choose the smoothest story. (13) Future researchers can learn not only what residents remember but also where memories diverge. (14) Beginning now will not recover every missing name, but delay can only narrow what remains possible.",
      "source": "Original passage created for AP Exam Practice."
    },
