      "text": "(1) The town's outdoor-lighting rule limits brightness but says little about direction. (2) As a result, a compliant fixture may send substantial light sideways or upward, where it produces glare and brightens the night sky. (3) The town should require new publicly funded fixtures to be fully shielded and should replace existing unshielded fixtures as they fail.\n\n(4) Shielding is not the same as dimming every street. (5) A shield directs light toward the ground, allowing planners to illuminate a walkway without shining into bedroom windows or drivers' eyes. (6) Fixture height, spacing, and color temperature still require site-specific judgment.\n\n(7) An immediate townwide replacement would waste working equipment and strain the capital budget. (8) A replacement-at-failure policy avoids that expense while steadily improving the system. (9) Priority retrofits should be considered at intersections and public buildings where residents have documented glare.\n\n(10) The policy should include an exception process for playing fields and emergency sites. (11) Exceptions should be written, time-limited, and reviewed rather than assumed. (12) By controlling direction first and replacing equipment gradually, the town can improve visibility, reduce spill light, and spend predictably.",
